artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the problem of predicting nuclear ground-state masses (or equivalently mass excesses / binding energies) across the nuclear chart by means of a supervised machine-learning model (for example a feed-forward neural network, Gaussian-process emulator, kernel ridge regression, or symbolic regressor) trained on a subset of the experimentally known masses. Let T denote a training/validation set of measured nuclei and let E denote an "extrapolation" set of measured nuclei deliberately excluded from T. A typical observation is the following: if one trains a neural-network mass model on all known nuclei with Z < 82 and tests it on the 508 known masses with Z ≥ 82, the root-mean-square deviation on the excluded heavy region can reach about 37.8 MeV (with individual deviations up to ~120 MeV near Z = 110), even though the global rms over all 2550 known masses remains around 16.9 MeV and the interpolation error inside the training region is small. Formulate and justify a machine-learning methodology (architecture, choice of input features, loss function, regularization, uncertainty quantification, and/or hybridization with a physical mass model) that simultaneously:

(i) preserves or improves the in-sample (interpolation) accuracy on the training/validation region;
(ii) yields a controlled, quantitatively bounded rms deviation when extrapolating to nuclei outside the training region, in particular to heavy nuclei with Z ≥ 82 and toward the proton/neutron drip lines and the superheavy region;
(iii) provides reliable, calibrated predictive uncertainties on its extrapolated predictions;
(iv) reduces computational cost relative to direct evaluation of microscopic nuclear models (i.e., acts as an efficient emulator usable for large-scale parameter scans).

Specifically: does there exist such a machine-learning scheme for nuclear mass prediction that demonstrably satisfies (i)–(iv), and if so, characterize the minimal physics input (symmetries, asymptotic behaviour, theoretical mass-model priors, or domain-informed activation functions) required so that the extrapolation error to heavy/superheavy nuclei remains bounded by a prescribed tolerance?

